<%# The masthead every screen behind the gate wears: the nameplate and the
    ways off the page on one ruled band, the line naming this page on a
    second.

    The nav does not change shape from page to page, so the reader learns one
    navigation and everything is always one click away. The nameplate is the
    way back to the day's briefing, as a newspaper's is, which is what keeps
    an edition opened out of the archive from being a page with no way
    forward.

    Subscriptions is in the nav rather than reached only from the edition
    page's badge. The badge shows while something is held, and the two
    sections a reader wants when nothing is held — new senders, recently
    bounced — are the ones that answer "did the thing I subscribed to ever
    arrive?". A page reachable only while something is waiting could not
    answer that.

    Every page behind the gate wears this one, the archive of originals
    included — its own header was the read-state filter bar, and when that
    went there was nothing left in it but the nameplate. That is also what
    freed the block name: .masthead__brand belonged to the feed's header
    until this replaced it. %>
<header class="masthead">
  <div class="masthead__bar">
    <%= link_to root_path, class: "masthead__home" do %>
      <%= render "application/lockup", size: 24 %>
    <% end %>

    <nav class="masthead__nav">
      <%= link_to t("application.masthead.archive"), editions_path,
            class: "masthead__link" %>
      <%= link_to t("application.masthead.originals"), newsletters_path,
            class: "masthead__link" %>
      <%= link_to t("application.masthead.subscriptions"), subscriptions_path,
            class: "masthead__link" %>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <%# Set in mono, so uppercased by CSS rather than shouted in the locale
      file. %>
  <h1 class="masthead__line"><%= line %></h1>
</header>
